ba9b31a88e34e7c68dee8739d631a6a8b779388","commits":[],"cumulative_patch_sha256":"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855","finding_commits":{},"parent":"5ba9b31a88e34e7c68dee8739d631a6a8b779388","patch_sha256":"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855","tree":"be1a607b8a4a08533ab5c54b0174301afc4901a5"},"dispositions":{"comments":[{"body":"Replacing `transform` with `onComplete` leaves the returned promise unresolved when the dispatcher rejects the callback. This is possible with Pekko's supported `affinity-pool-executor` when its bounded queue overflows: Scala reports the `RejectedExecutionException` without running `EndSpanHandler`, so neither its `finally` block nor `promise.complete` executes. I reproduced a completed handler response leaving the replacement future pending even after the dispatcher recovered; the former `transform` future completed with a failure instead. Preserve the root-context isolation while ensuring callback-scheduling failures also complete the returned future, and cover a rejecting dispatcher.","line":105,"path":"instrumentation/pekko/pekko-http-1.0/javaagent/src/main/java/io/opentelemetry/javaagent/instrumentation/pekkohttp/v1_0/server/PekkoHttpServerHandlerWrapper.java","side":"RIGHT"}],"input_identity":{"pr_diff_sha256":"fd9bbe16504f99f783c4f9d0937dd6f1ead3eb3bc819e15828b121e2e9e8ed86"},"outcome":"comments","request_digest":"228b50e9f9ba56f349e004354a751d318844be0e06ec2177bc3a00c78a467b1a","schema":2},"download_sha256":"518f71bcfdc63233e1f0dcf6c4bb64064249543fd3f0e83412ddac0acde00909","frozen_sha":"5ba9b31a88e34e7c68dee8739d631a6a8b779388","pr":20497,"publication_eligible":false,"repo":"open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-instrumentation","request_digest":"228b50e9f9ba56f349e004354a751d318844be0e06ec2177bc3a00c78a467b1a","request_id":"a544d47d9cfe4303bc8ba7daad897a5e","run_attempt":1,"run_id":39159489119,"schema":2,"scope":"Structural/Git verification only. No target code or tests are exec